---
import ToolLayout from '@/layouts/ToolLayout.astro'
import JpgToPdf from '@/components/tools/pdf-convert/JpgToPdf.tsx'
---

<ToolLayout slug="jpg-to-pdf">
  <JpgToPdf client:load />

  <Fragment slot="explainer">
    <h2>How to convert JPG images to a PDF</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Drop your photos onto the box above, or click to browse. Select as many as you need. Your device decodes them, and they are not uploaded.</li>
      <li>Put them in order. Drag a card to another position, or use the arrow buttons on each card, which also work with the keyboard.</li>
      <li>Choose a page size. <strong>Auto</strong> gives each page the exact shape of its image. A4 or Letter gives all pages the same dimensions.</li>
      <li>For a fixed page size, set the orientation, margin and fit mode.</li>
      <li>Press <strong>Create PDF</strong>, then download. Change an option and press <strong>Rebuild</strong> to try again without adding the images again.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2>Fit modes, in plain terms</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Mode</th>
            <th>What it does</th>
            <th>Side effect</th>
            <th>Use it when</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Fit</td>
            <td>Scales the image down until it fits inside the margins</td>
            <td>White space on the shorter side</td>
            <td>Almost always. Nothing is lost or distorted</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Fill</td>
            <td>Scales up until the page is fully covered</td>
            <td>The edges that overflow are cropped</td>
            <td>You want edge-to-edge pages and can lose the borders</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Stretch</td>
            <td>Forces the image to the exact page box</td>
            <td>The aspect ratio changes. People look wider or thinner</td>
            <td>Rarely. Only when the source already has the correct shape</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Page size reference</h2>
    <div class="overflow-x-auto">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Page size</th>
            <th>Millimetres</th>
            <th>Inches</th>
            <th>PDF points</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>A5</td><td>148 × 210</td><td>5.83 × 8.27</td><td>421 × 595</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A4</td><td>210 × 297</td><td>8.27 × 11.69</td><td>595 × 842</td></tr>
          <tr><td>A3</td><td>297 × 420</td><td>11.69 × 16.54</td><td>842 × 1191</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Letter</td><td>216 × 279</td><td>8.5 × 11</td><td>612 × 792</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Legal</td><td>216 × 356</td><td>8.5 × 14</td><td>612 × 1008</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tabloid</td><td>279 × 432</td><td>11 × 17</td><td>792 × 1224</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>Is the image quality reduced?</h2>
    <p>
      No. A JPG is already compressed with a codec that PDF supports natively, so
      the tool copies the file's bytes into the document. There is no decode and
      re-encode step, so there is no generation loss. The page is as sharp as the
      original photo, and the PDF is about the sum of the input file sizes.
    </p>
    <p>
      There is one exception. Phone cameras often store a photo sideways and add an
      EXIF orientation tag that tells viewers to rotate it. PDF has no equivalent
      tag, so that image would show on its side. When the tool finds a rotated tag,
      it applies the rotation and saves again at quality 94. The result looks the
      same, but it is technically a re-encode.
    </p>

    <h2>What people use this for</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Expense claims: a folder of receipt photos becomes one PDF that finance systems accept.</li>
      <li>Applications and ID: passport, licence and certificate scans in one attachment.</li>
      <li>Portfolios and proofs: a set of images as one paginated, printable document.</li>
      <li>Homework and handouts: photographed pages that a teacher can annotate.</li>
      <li>Archiving: a shoot in one file with consistent page dimensions.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      The JPG to PDF conversion runs in your browser, so documents with names,
      addresses, card numbers or medical details are not sent to a third party. For
      a mixed batch of formats, use <a href="/images-to-pdf">Images to PDF</a>, which
      also accepts PNG and WebP.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
